 demonstrates that people  support the direction of travel support  the work the government's trying to do  i'm really pleased with this paper it's  not the answer but it is the it's the  direction we need to go and it gives a  clear route map to reforming a system  that's long overdue reform yeah as you  said important to get the details right  in this area ms hancock thank you for  coming on today because um you will be  well aware that one of our top stories  is about those fixed penalty notices  handed out um to government staff  members for parties at downing street  and whitehall this is a clear sign  that the law was broken  well we haven't seen that yet from the  from the met police have we we haven't  heard the news of  of whether those have been issued we've  just been told that they will be the  look well they only issue the fixed  penalty notices if covered rules covered  laws were broken  well the  the pm has apologized and you know the  question that we've got that faces us  today  is do we want to change prime minister  in the middle of a war i haven't even  got the prime minister not only  a second i haven't even gotten to that  question yet i know i know that it's  coming though i know that it's coming no  i know but let's not pre-judge things  let's not pre-judge things i just want  to go through this because you know we  are going over old ground but what i'm  talking about is fixed penalty notices  are issued when a law has been broken  but what we're struggling with today is  that number 10 and nobody from the  conservatives will actually come out and  hold your hands up and go the met police  have found us guilty of breaking the law  why not well we haven't seen i haven't  seen that d because i haven't seen that  detail yet and the met haven't published  that they think they'll be issuing 20  people  but they haven't given any detail i'm  certainly not privy to it um but the  number 10 uh team have said that if the  prime minister is issued with such a  notice then uh then they will make that  public okay then he will have been found  to break the law is that correct  well  the thing is that you know this this  issue has been gone over a huge amount i  understand of course i understand  the  the concern the frustration and the  emotions that people have when  when faced with these this sort of um  allegations  then if a fixed penalty notice is issued  yeah that is an indication of breaking  the law so if the prime minister gets  one of those he has break on the law is  that correct  well it depends what happens but the  real question okay is do what where do  we go from here and the prime minister  has apologized  he understands and i certainly  understand  the  the the the anger and frustration that  people  feel about this issue but the real  question that we've got and this is why  i address this you know my first  response to your question the real  question we've got is what do we do  about it now okay and what we do about  it now is we need to move forward  thankfully we're one of the first  countries in the world out of covert  restrictions in large part down to the  uh speed of the vaccination rollout  which we delivered here  than any other i get that but the  question is you're saying down to the  pm's judgment yeah again what happens  now i mean it's almost like you're  glossing over it in terms of if the  prime minister is found to have broken  the law and you know those people at  downing downing street parties and white  hole parties have been found to break  the law otherwise they wouldn't be  issued with fixed penalty notices if  laws have been broken and if the prime  minister misled parliament by saying and  i quote here back in december to secure  stamina um their uh all guidance was  completely followed in number 10 he  repeatedly assured the house there were  no parties so if he misled parliament  lying in the words of labour are we just  to pretend there are no consequences to  those actions  and we just move on  the prime minister's the prime  minister's apologize but but it is  really important you know when you're as  as an mp as a representative of my  constituents  you know if  once the prime minister has apologized  the idea that you should  that he should have to resign  following something like this  that question has to take into account  everything that's going on  because this isn't the only subject it  is an important subject of course it is  and we've just been talking about  another important subject and then  there's some huge subjects facing the  country like the war and then there's  the economic uh situation as well do you  get that  that will really upset people watching  because yes of course there are other  things going on but to those people we  saw at that memorial in central london  today at the memorial um wall opposite  where you're standing now you can see it  from the house of parliament to those  people it really does matter and you  know in their eyes if the prime minister  broke the law and lied he should face  consequences and apology doesn't cut it  you can't just confuse it with the war  in ukraine the brexit issue you can't  confuse it  i'm not confusing anything i'm being  really clear which is that  the question of who should be prime  minister has to take everything into  account and he has apologized for this  he's made changes in the number 10  operation but you've got to ask the  question  do we want to change prime minister now  no  who do we want to be leading the country  when  when the when boris johnson is not only  providing leadership to this country  frankly he's providing leadership  with with colleagues and friends and  allies around the world in tackling the  threat of vladimir putin which is a  threat to us all it's not just a threat  to ukraine but it's a threat that  undermines the the the  the safe liberal world order that we've  been living in um and so there are  huge questions  about u